10. Security Domains

MOS security is divided into domains.

10.1 Identity Security

Protect:

identities,
credentials,
authentication.
10.2 Memory Security

Protect:

facts,
experiences,
private knowledge.
10.3 Communication Security

Protect:

messages,
synchronization,
delegation.
10.4 Execution Security

Protect:

tools,
actions,
environments.
10.5 Resource Security

Protect:

compute,
money,
infrastructure.
11. Memory Access Control

Memory requires granular access.

Examples:

Private Memory

↓

User Controlled


Shared Memory

↓

Organization Controlled


Public Knowledge

↓

Open Access
12. Cognitive Data Classification

MOS introduces information classification.

Public

Safe to share.

Internal

Available inside trusted systems.

Restricted

Requires permission.

Confidential

Requires explicit authorization.

Secret

Highest protection.

13. Action Risk Model

Actions are evaluated by risk.

Risk factors:

impact,
reversibility,
cost,
uncertainty,
authority required.

Example:

Low Risk:

Summarize document

High Risk:

Transfer money
14. Human Oversight Model

Some operations require humans.

Approval triggers:

high financial impact,
irreversible action,
security changes,
privacy-sensitive operations.

Example:

Agent:

"I want to delete 50GB of data."


Policy:

Human approval required.
15. Autonomous Boundaries

MOS allows configurable autonomy levels.

Level 0

No autonomy.

Human performs actions.

Level 1

Recommendation only.

Level 2

Execute approved tasks.

Level 3

Autonomous execution within boundaries.

Level 4

Self-directed operation with monitoring.

16. Security Events

Security events are immutable.

Examples:

login attempt,
permission change,
denied action,
policy violation.
17. Governance Learning

RFC-0011 allows governance improvement.

MOS can learn:

unnecessary approvals,
repeated violations,
better policies.

However:

Governance changes require stronger validation than normal learning.

18. Policy Versioning

Policies evolve.

Example:

Production Deployment Policy v1

↓

Production Deployment Policy v2

Historical policies remain available.

19. Accountability Chain

Every action SHALL preserve:

Who

↓

Requested

↓

Approved

↓

Executed

↓

Changed

↓

Evaluated
20. Explainability Requirement

Every restricted action SHALL answer:

Who performed it?
Under what authority?
Which policy allowed it?
Which constraints applied?
What was the outcome?
21. Governance Graph

MOS introduces a Governance Graph.

Example:

Human Owner

      │

      ▼

Organization

      │

      ▼

Agent

      │

      ▼

Capability

      │

      ▼

Permission

      │

      ▼

